  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 326 AM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nEarly morning GOES WV imagery shows the mid level ridge extending \ninto the Upper Mississippi River Valley, pressing the downstream \ntrough over the Lower Great Lakes and along the province line of \nOntario and Quebec eastward. Surface high pressure ridging extending \nfrom James Bay across much of the Mississippi River Valley is \nsupporting the calm conditions within the CWA. SPC mesoanalysis \nindicates the entire CWA is under less than 1 inch of PWATs with \nvalues as low as 0.5 inches over the east half. This and clear skies \nhave allowed radiative cooling processes to lower temps into the 50s \nto mid 40s so far. Forecasted lows in the 40s to mid 50s, coldest \ninterior remain on track.\n\nDry weather continues through tonight as the ridge builds into the \nGreat Lakes and sfc high pressure becomes confined to the Lower \nGreat Lakes/OH River Valley. Meanwhile a shortwave trough over the \nnorthern Rockies tracks to Manitoba and western Ontario by Tuesday \nmorning, increasing sfc troughing from the west which bears a cold \nfront. The increasing southwest flow warms 850 mb temps to 18-22 C \nby late tonight, and with 500 mb height rises yields a warm up with \nwidespread highs in the upper 70s to upper 80s. Lows stay mild \ntonight in the upper 50s to upper 60s.\n\nThe shortwave trough swings through northwestern Ontario on Tuesday, \ndriving a cold front and weak surface trough across the UP. Better \ndynamic forcing remains to the north of the UP and instability is \ncapped much of the day, generally peaking around 1000 J/kg. \nDespite PWATs to between 1.5 to 1.8 inches with this boundary, \nmodel soundings are lack luster for deep moisture and the quick \nnature leaves much to desire for rainfall amounts. Even with \nsufficient deep-layer shear, strong to severe storms seem \nunlikely. As a result, the forecast reflects 20-40% PoPs from \nwest to east on Tuesday. NBM probabilities of at least 0.25 \ninches are only between 10-40% over the east half of the UP and \n0Z HREF probabilities struggle to even reach 10-15% just about \nanywhere within the CWA.\n\nTemperatures fall back into the 70s on Wednesday behind the front, \nbut look to quickly rebound back into the 80s on Thursday as \nadditional systems bring more chances for precipitation from Friday \ninto the weekend.",
